        <h2 id="faq" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How many times can I counter a settlement offer?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              There is no legal limit on how many times you can counter an offer. Most claims go through 2 to 5 rounds of back-and-forth before settling. Each counter should move toward a mutually acceptable number while being fully supported by your documentation. If you have been negotiating for several rounds and the insurer is not moving meaningfully, that is a signal to consult an attorney about escalating to a formal demand letter or filing suit.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Can I negotiate directly with the insurance company without a lawyer?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Yes — for minor accidents with clear liability and modest injuries, self-represented claimants can negotiate reasonable settlements. However, for moderate to serious injuries, having an attorney almost always results in significantly higher net recovery even after their contingency fee. Attorneys know the claim's true value, recognize adjuster tactics, and have the leverage of potential litigation behind them. Most personal injury attorneys offer free consultations, which costs you nothing even if you ultimately decide to proceed alone.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              What if the insurance company refuses to negotiate at all?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              If an insurer flatly refuses to negotiate in good faith — denying a clearly covered claim without a legitimate basis or refusing to move from an unreasonably low offer — this may constitute insurance bad faith. You can file a complaint with your state's Department of Insurance, which can investigate and sanction the insurer. More powerfully, you can file a lawsuit. The threat and reality of litigation almost always brings unreasonable insurers to the table.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Should I negotiate with my own insurance company or the other driver's?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              In a fault state, your primary negotiation is with the at-fault driver's liability insurer. You negotiate with your own insurer for property damage (collision coverage), medical bills (MedPay or PIP), and any Underinsured Motorist (UIM) claim if the other driver's limits are insufficient. In a no-fault state, you file your medical and wage claims with your own insurer first, regardless of fault. The at-fault driver's insurer becomes relevant only if your injuries meet the state's "injury threshold" to step outside the no-fault system.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How long does negotiation take before a settlement is reached?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              The negotiation phase itself — from sending the demand letter to signing the settlement release — typically takes 1 to 3 months for moderate injury claims. Simpler claims with clear liability and minor injuries can wrap up in 3 to 6 weeks. More complex cases, or those involving disputed liability or serious injuries, can stretch the negotiation phase to 4 to 6 months or more. Read our full guide on <a href="/blog/how-long-does-settlement-take/" class="text-link hover:underline">how long car accident settlements take</a> for a complete timeline breakdown.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <p>
          Negotiating a car accident settlement is not about being aggressive — it is about being prepared, patient, and persistent. Know your numbers, build your evidence, and refuse to let an insurance adjuster's low offer be the final word. You have the right to full and fair compensation, and the tools in this guide will help you get it.
        </p>

